export default "Apple Health and Google Health Connect serve a real purpose. They aggregate health data from multiple apps into a single place, making it easier to share a consolidated health picture with doctors or track changes over time. For many types of health data, this is a straightforward tradeoff.\n\nMenstrual cycle data sits in different legal and political territory. In states where abortion access is restricted or prosecuted, menstrual cycle data has potential use as evidence in legal proceedings. The data consolidated in Apple Health and Google Health Connect is subject to subpoena and law enforcement requests governed by US law, which provides fewer protections for health data than many users assume.\n\nThe apps in this list take the position that menstrual cycle data should not flow into platform health aggregators by default. That is either a deliberate design decision (Floriva, Drip, Euki) or a consequence of no integration existing (Read Your Body).\n\nThe distinction between \"no health platform sync\" and \"fully offline\" matters. Drip is the only app in this list that makes zero network connections. Floriva uses its own encrypted sync infrastructure. Euki is on-device only. Read Your Body offers user-controlled cloud backup that is separate from health data stores.\n\nFor most users, Floriva is the best recommendation because it keeps period data away from Apple and Google while still supporting a more complete daily tracker. Drip and Euki are good options if you use one phone and want the narrowest possible local-only setup. If you need data accessible across devices, Floriva provides that without routing through Apple or Google.";
